
Non-exhaustive list of content I've read/seen that I liked, sometimes followed with a small write up about why I liked it. The list is a mix of everything related to software engineering: from _people_ to _processes_ and uh, well, software:

- *[The Consensus](https://theconsensus.dev/about.html)*: I really like the approach to a software bulletin and it's focus on "hardcore" software.

- *[My AI Adoption Journey](https://mitchellh.com/writing/my-ai-adoption-journey)*: I really like the sensible take he has on the whole AI debate thingy. It really helps the case that the man is him.

> _"I really don't care one way or the other if AI is here to stay, I'm a software craftsman that just wants to build stuff for the love of the game."_

- *[Fallthrough](https://fallthrough.fm/)*: I like how the conversations feel human and they are not a covered advertisement. 

- I really liked these because they encourage you to think rather than blindly follow advice.

    - *[Choose Boring Technology](https://mcfunley.com/choose-boring-technology)*.
    - *[Java for Everything](https://www.teamten.com/lawrence/writings/java-for-everything.html)*.
    - *[The one ring problem: abstraction and our quest for power](https://www.tedinski.com/2018/01/30/the-one-ring-problem-abstraction-and-power.html)*: Everything by Ted Kaminski is really good.

- *[You Might Be Better Off Without Pull Requests](https://hamvocke.com/blog/better-off-without-pull-requests/)*.

- *[Test, don't (just) verify](https://alperenkeles.com/posts/test-dont-verify/)*.
- *[My Kind of REPL](https://ianthehenry.com/posts/my-kind-of-repl/)*.
- *[Notes on structured concurrency, or: Go statement considered harmful](https://vorpus.org/blog/notes-on-structured-concurrency-or-go-statement-considered-harmful)*.

- *[Why async Rust?](https://without.boats/blog/why-async-rust/)*.

- *[Reality has a surprising amount of detail](https://johnsalvatier.org/blog/2017/reality-has-a-surprising-amount-of-detail)*

- Sensible advice for career development:

    - *[Value over replacement in software engineering](https://www.seangoedecke.com/value-over-replacement/)*.
    - *[Don't Call Yourself A Programmer, And Other Career Advice](https://www.kalzumeus.com/2011/10/28/dont-call-yourself-a-programmer/)*: Even when I don't believe that the title “programmer” means what it says there, *it is* a well-written article about the reality of the profession and what we should keep focus on first, loving computers it's hardly the reason one is employed.
    - *[How to ask good questions](https://jvns.ca/blog/good-questions/)*
    - *[Get your work recognized: write a brag document](https://jvns.ca/blog/brag-documents/)*.
    - *[Cheerleading](https://notes.eatonphil.com/2025-05-26-cheerleading.html)*: *I am* someone whose mindset is to think that what I can share doesn't have much value, so this kind of advice is good because I wouldn't think of it. 
    - *[Publishing your work increases your luck ](https://github.com/readme/guides/publishing-your-work)*: It's all about being present and having a signal.
    - *[About 40 hours](https://thesquareplanet.com/blog/about-40-hours/)*
